t to sit  here in 2020 and think how this is going  to be in 2025  when it's removing so rapidly when  technologies like hydrogen that didn't  seem viable  only a couple of years ago suddenly  seemed more so now  and i'd like to bring in a question  again from nigel freeman saying what  about things like  um tidal power and will the government  be doing research and development  in that because who knows by about five  years time we may be talking about that  the green agenda does seem to surprise  us quite  you talk about it as if it's some sort  of act of god i mean people have decided  that that's what we want to do  it wasn't something that has just  evolved like you know from adam smith's  invisible hand i mean there's  been policy decisions that have made  these things happen  um you know when i came in uh to the  office at the beginning of this year  um after the christmas break i said to  my officials hydrogen is going to be a  big story let's  look at that and we've been looking at  it and we've been driving the agenda so  it's something that  we can control it's not something that's  happened to us  and i think uh and it was in nigel who  said about um  uh tidal power we absolutely need to  look at tidal power  many of the projects that i've seen i'm  afraid haven't been uh as cost-effective  as they might have been  but that's definitely something that  we're looking at and if we get  get it right we'll get the auction  process right we can incentivize  investment um we can we can score a  success  uh in that too i just wanted to resist  this idea that  somehow the green revolution is  happening um  as though it were an act of nature it's  no it's not it's something that  you know takes a lot of policy a lot of  business entrepreneurship  a lot of innovation a lot of research um  and it all comes together in sometimes  uh things  lead to a dead end but um a lot of the  time you know these things are are being  willed  uh in a deliberate uh in a deliberate